1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 c5 3. d5 e6 4. Nc3 exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. e4 g6 7. Nf3 Bg7 8. Bd3
O-O 9. O-O (9. h3 $1 {This is widely regarded as an excellent setup for White now,
and ...} 9... b5 $1 {...is highly theoretical.}) 9... Na6 $6 {..Bg4 should be
played to prevent the Nd2 $1 maneuver. In general, Black is happy to exchange the
light-square bisohp for the knight, gaining dark-square influence in the
center.} (9... Bg4 $1 $12) 10. Nd2 Nb4 {The knight LOOKS good here, but it does
nothing.} 11. Be2 (11. Bb1 $1 $16 {This would have contained the knight more
effectively and kept an eye on possible kingside actions.}) 11... Re8 12. a3 Na6
13. Re1 Nc7 14. Qc2 Rb8 15. a4 b6 16. Nb5 $2 {This maneuver makes no sense to me.
What is it's purpose $2 The tempos spent and the exchange of the knight on c7 only
aids Tal.} 16... a6 17. Nxc7 (17. Na7 {This is preferred by Tal, but it's an odd
preference as after the line he gives, Black is better than in the game.} 17...
Bb7 18. Nc6 Bxc6 19. dxc6 d5 $1 $17) 17... Qxc7 18. Ra2 Qe7 19. f3 $2 {This proves to
be a massive error. The move accomplishes nothing and blocks the bishop on e3.
Tal responds powerfully.} (19. b3 {placing the bishop on b2 gives White a
reasonable position.} 19... Nxe4 20. Bd3 f5 21. Nxe4 fxe4 22. Rxe4 Qf7 23. Rxe8+
Qxe8 $15) 19... Nh5 $1 {When your opponent makes a move, ask yourself what they've
left undefended, and try to exploit that.} 20. Nf1 f5 $1 21. Bd3 f4 $1 {The plan of
...Be5, and ...g5-g4 with a mating attack is deadly.} 22. g4 $1 {Birbrager tries
to reverse the course of the game, but Tal responds aggressively and well. A
lesser player might have lost the advantage by exchanging or retreating the
knight here.} 22... Bd4+ 23. Kh1 (23. Kg2 $2 Qh4 24. Re2 Bxg4 $1 25. fxg4 Qxg4+ 26.
Kh1 Qg1#) 23... Qh4 24. Re2 Qh3 $2 {This ends up being magnificent, but ...Bxg4 $1
was objectively stronger.} (24... Bxg4 $1 25. fxg4 f3 26. gxh5 fxe2 27. Qxe2 c4 $1
{Maybe Tal missed this $2 It's my best rationale for why he didn't play this way.}
28. Bb1 Rf8 $19) 25. Rg2 Qxf3 26. Nd2 (26. gxh5 Rxe4 $1 $19 {is a beautiful and
punishing blow.}) 26... Qe3 27. Nf1 Qf3 28. Nd2 {draw $2} 28... Bxg4 $3 {No draw $1
Tal chooses to sacrifice his queen instead $1 This is a perfect example of a
\"Tal\" sacrifice; there is not a clear idea to regain the material, but Tal's
pieces are alive and crackling with energy while White's pieces struggle to find
meaning in the position. Objectively, MAYBE White can defend, but there's no
clear refutation, and White collapses almost immediately.} 29. Nxf3 Bxf3 30. h4
{This move gives the king room to run and is aimed to respond to the very
dangerous threat of ...Re5 $1, ...Ng3+ $1, and ...Rh5# However, ...Re5 hasn't been
played yet. Maybe the tempo could have been better used $2} (30. Be2 $2 Rxe4 $1 $19)
(30. h3 $6 Rxe4 31. Bxe4 Ng3+ 32. Kh2 Nf1+ 33. Kh1 Ng3+ $12 {is a funny draw. Tal
might not take it though.}) (30. b3 {I like this move, improving mobility on the
second rank, and considering Bb2.} 30... Re5 31. h4 $13) (30. Qd2 $5 {This intends
Be2 which may resolve some of the pressure.} 30... Re5 31. h4 Bxe4 32. Bxe4 Rxe4
$13 {Even Stockfish thinks Black is doing fine now.}) (30. Bd2 $5 {Tal thought this
was White's best. To me, b3 feels like a better version of this though because
it also improves the Ra2.}) 30... Rf8 31. Be2 $4 {White has a similar choice as
he did on the last move, but under great pressure, he concedes a winning
endgame, becoming the first of many victims to a famous Tal queen sacrifice.}
31... Ng3+ 32. Kh2 Bxg2 33. Kxg2 Nxe2 34. Qxe2 f3+ {\"fork town\" - population
Birbrager} 35. Qxf3 Rxf3 36. Kxf3 Rf8+ 37. Kg3 Be5+ 38. Kg2 Bf4 {...Rf4 (before
or after trading on c1) wins another pawn and the game.} 0-1